Chapter: LirFrameCross

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The shapes that make the ARM64 LIR selector build a frame
 (`codex/plugs/arm64/Arm64Lir.codex`, step 4d). Step 4c emitted straight-line
 arithmetic with no prologue and no epilogue, so it declined any function whose
 allocation reached a spill slot or a callee-saved register. These are the
 functions that reach them.

 ARM64 gives the allocator eighteen registers: nine callee-saved, one
 non-crosser preference, and the eight argument registers. A leaf therefore
 reaches the callee-saved pool only at ten simultaneously live values and
 spills only at nineteen, which is why an ordinary two- or three-parameter
 expression cannot exercise either path and these shapes are as large as they
 are. The counts were read off `build/compile.ps1 -Passes lir-dump` rather than
 guessed: `saved` peaks at eleven live values and `spilled` at twenty.

 Every shape here is order-sensitive. `saved` and `spilled` subtract at each
 join, so a selector that swapped an ALU operand, loaded a spill slot into the
 wrong scratch, or restored a callee-saved register over a live value produces
 a different number rather than the same one. `regs-only` is the control: it
 fits entirely in registers, takes no frame, and must answer exactly what step
 4c already answered for it.

 `unused-first` and `swapped` are the parameter cases. Neither can displace a
 parameter on this target, and they are here to hold that ground: an unused
 leading parameter still occupies its own argument register, and a pair read in
 reverse is a reversed read rather than a reversed allocation. If a future
 construct ever does displace a parameter, these are the shapes that were
 already known not to.

 `via-call` calls the framed definitions from a tree-emitted function, so the
 offsets and arities the selector registers have to be right for an ordinary
 call to land on a function that moves the stack pointer and puts it back.

Section: Frame Shapes

  regs-only : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  regs-only (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) =
   a + b - c + d - e + f - g + h

  saved : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  saved (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) =
   ((a * b) - (c * d)) + (((e * f) - (g * h)) + (((a + c) - (e + g)) + ((b + d) - (f + h))))

  spilled : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spilled (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) =
   (a * b) - ((c * d) + ((e * f) - ((g * h) + ((a * c) - ((b * d) + ((e * g) - ((f * h) + ((a * d) - ((b * c) + ((e * h) - ((f * g) + (((a - b) + (c - d)) + ((e - f) + (g - h))))))))))))))

  unused-first : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  unused-first (a) (b) (c) = b * c - b

  swapped : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  swapped (a) (b) = b - a

  via-call : Integer -> Integer
  via-call (n) = saved n 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 - swapped n 4

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("regs-only: " & show (regs-only 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))
    print-line-uni ("saved: " & show (saved 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))
    print-line-uni ("saved-perm: " & show (saved 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 1))
    print-line-uni ("spilled: " & show (spilled 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))
    print-line-uni ("spilled-perm: " & show (spilled 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 1))
    print-line-uni ("unused-first: " & show (unused-first 99 6 4))
    print-line-uni ("swapped: " & show (swapped 9 4))
    print-line-uni ("via-call: " & show (via-call 10))
  end
